Title:
AI-Assisted Vulnerability Research in Silverpeas (CVE-2026-53695 to CVE-2026-53700)

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This write-up documents an AI-assisted source-code review of the Silverpeas collaboration platform, resulting in six CVEs spanning XSS, account takeover, arbitrary file read, and multiple paths to RCE. The author used LLM agents (primarily Gemini CLI, secondarily Claude) to enumerate attack surfaces and trace data flows, then manually validated exploitability and impact in a local Docker/WildFly deployment. Key findings include an unauthenticated reflected XSS that can steal an admin API token, a legacy unauthenticated password-change flow enabling two-request admin takeover, and upload/path traversal bugs that allow writing a malicious WAR into WildFly’s auto-deploy directory for code execution. The post emphasizes that LLMs provided breadth (finding candidate sinks/paths) but required an experienced operator to eliminate false positives and escalate impact (e.g., turning file write into RCE via deployment mechanics). Useful for offensive security researchers, pentesters, and AppSec teams interested in real-world Java web app bug classes and practical AI-assisted review workflows.

Technical Focus:
- Java/JSP sink analysis and context-appropriate output encoding (HTML attribute vs JS string)
- Authentication/password reset logic flaws and legacy/deprecated endpoint exposure
- Path traversal and arbitrary file write via upload handlers and session identifiers
- WildFly/JBoss deployment auto-discovery abuse (WAR drop + .dodeploy) for RCE
- Access control bypass patterns in file-serving servlets (absolute path handling)

Use Cases:
- Identify and validate XSS sinks in JSP applications beyond naive blocklists
- Test password reset/change flows for missing identity binding and legacy endpoints
- Exploit upload/path traversal to achieve arbitrary file write and escalate to RCE on WildFly
- Assess file download/servlet handlers for absolute-path reads and weak authorization gates
- Build an AI-assisted code review workflow with manual exploit-path confirmation

Keywords:
Silverpeas, CVE-2026-53695, CVE-2026-53696, CVE-2026-53697, CVE-2026-53698, CVE-2026-53699, CVE-2026-53700, reflected XSS, HTML attribute injection, JavaScript string injection, API token theft, account takeover, password reset bypass, legacy endpoint, path traversal, arbitrary file write, file upload, arbitrary file read, WildFly, WAR deployment, .dodeploy, JBoss, Docker, JSP, servlet, FileServer, upload session, X-UPLOAD-SESSION